{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-213094",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "If a relation $R$ is defined over $A={{a}_{1},{a}_{2},…,{a}_{m}}$ and $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},…{b}_{n}}$ of cardinalities $m$ and $n,$ respectively, the *adjacency matrix* of $R$ is a $m\\times n$-matrix (or table), where the element ${r}_{ij}$ at the intersection of the $i$th row and the $j$th column is given by\n\n$$\n{r}_{ij}={\\begin{aligned}1,\\,\\text{if }{a}_{i}\\,R\\,{b}_{j}, \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given ordered pairs.\n\n- The pair $(✠,♣)$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the adjacency matrix has $1$ on the intersection of the $✠$-row and the $♣$-column.\n\n- The pair $(♣,♠)$ does not belong to the relation since the adjacency matrix has $0$ on the intersection of the $♣$-row and the $♠$-column.\n\n- The pair $(♠,✠)$ does not belong to the relation since the adjacency matrix has $0$ on the intersection of the $♠$-row and the $✠$-column.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
